Q: Java: Count empty lines in a text file/string A: <h2>Modern Java</h2>
<p>Much easier using the features of modern Java: NIO.2, streams, lambdas, method references, ‘IO’ class, etc.</p>
<p>Use <code>Path.of</code> to represent file location.</p>
<p>Open the file by getting a stream of lines via <code>Files.lines</code>.</p>
<p>Filter the stream of lines by examining each line to see if it has no characters (<code>isEmpty</code>), or if the line has only whitespace characters (<a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs//api/java.base/java/lang/String.html#isBlank()" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>isBlank</code></a>).</p>
<p>Finally, ask the stream for a count of those empty or blank lines, and dump to console.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>try 
    ( 
        Stream &lt; String &gt; lines = Files.lines( Path.of( &quot;path/to/example.txt&quot; ) ) ; 
    )
{ 
    long count = 
        lines
            .filter( line -&gt; line.isBlank() )  // Or `.isEmpty`. 
            .count() ;
    IO.println( count ) ;
}
catch …
</code></pre>
